@page "/components/scanning"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Codes

<DocsHeader Title="Scanning" Eyebrow="Capture">
    <Lead>Read barcodes and QR codes with the camera, a handheld scanner or the keyboard.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    Both components raise <code>OnScan</code> with a <code>StilScanResult</code>: the <code>Value</code>, the
    <code>Format</code> the camera saw (for example <code>qr_code</code> or <code>ean_13</code>; null otherwise) and the
    <code>Source</code> (<code>Camera</code>, <code>Scanner</code> or <code>Keyboard</code>). Every way in ends in the
    same handler, so a check-in or delivery screen works on a phone, at a desk with a USB scanner, and with only a
    keyboard. Both need an interactive render mode.
</p>

<h2 id="camera">With the camera</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Scanner)">
    <p>
        <code>StilScanner</code> shows the camera with a frame to aim at, reads every code in view, plays a short sound
        (<code>Sound</code>) and announces the code to screen readers. The same code is ignored for
        <code>Cooldown</code> milliseconds (1500), so holding it in view counts once. <code>Formats</code> limits what it
        reads, and it switches cameras and turns on the flashlight where the device has one. The field under the camera
        (<code>ManualEntry</code>) takes a typed code or a handheld scanner, and is all that shows where there is no
        camera.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h3 id="decoder">Browsers without a barcode reader</h3>
<p>
    stil.js reads codes with the browser's <code>BarcodeDetector</code>, which Chrome and Edge have on Android, ChromeOS
    and macOS. Safari, Firefox, and Chrome on Windows and Linux have none, and there the scanner shows only the field.
    Stil ships no third-party code, but you can register a decoder of your own: a function that takes the
    <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> and returns the codes in its current frame. This one uses ZXing; check its license, and
    host the file yourself if you prefer.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="javascript" Code="@DecoderSnippet" />
<p>
    Load it once, after Blazor, with <code>&lt;script type="module" src="scan-decoder.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>.
    A registered decoder is used instead of <code>BarcodeDetector</code> in every browser.
</p>

<h2 id="handheld">With a handheld scanner</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(ScanInput)">
    <p>
        A USB or Bluetooth scanner acts as a keyboard: it types the code very fast and presses Enter.
        <code>StilScanInput</code> raises <code>OnScan</code> on Enter and tells the two apart by how fast the keys came
        (<code>MaxKeyInterval</code>, 50 ms on average). Enter never submits the form, and the field empties for the next
        code (<code>ClearOnScan</code>). Put focus in it with <code>AutoFocus</code> on a screen made for scanning. It
        takes a label, a hint and validation like any field.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilScanner)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilScanInput)" />

@code {
    private const string DecoderSnippet = """
        // wwwroot/scan-decoder.js
        import { registerScanDecoder } from './_content/Stil/stil.js';
        import { BrowserMultiFormatReader, BarcodeFormat } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@zxing/browser@0.2.1/+esm';

        const reader = new BrowserMultiFormatReader();
        const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');

        registerScanDecoder(async video => {
            canvas.width = video.videoWidth;
            canvas.height = video.videoHeight;
            canvas.getContext('2d', { willReadFrequently: true }).drawImage(video, 0, 0);
            try {
                const result = reader.decodeFromCanvas(canvas);
                return [{ rawValue: result.getText(), format: BarcodeFormat[result.getBarcodeFormat()].toLowerCase() }];
            } catch {
                return []; // No code in this frame.
            }
        });
        """;
}
